### What is Event Notification?

Event Notification keeps the event as small as possible. The payload is an announcement: event ID, event type, aggregate ID, timestamp, and optionally a link. The consumer reacts by calling back to the producer to fetch the details it needs.

```java
package com.bank.event;

import java.time.Instant;

// Event Notification: tiny pointer
public record TransferNotification(
        String eventId,
        String eventType,
        String accountId,
        String detailsLink,
        Instant occurredAt
) {}

// Example payload:
// {
//   "eventId": "EVT-001",
//   "eventType": "TRANSFER_COMPLETED",
//   "accountId": "SAV2001",
//   "detailsLink": "/api/v1/transfers/TXN-123",
//   "occurredAt": "2025-01-15T10:30:00Z"
// }
// Payload size: ~180 bytes
```

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import com.bank.event.TransferNotification;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestClient;

@Component
public class NotificationConsumerWithCallback {

    private final RestClient restClient;

    public NotificationConsumerWithCallback(RestClient.Builder builder) {
        this.restClient = builder.baseUrl("http://transfer-service").build();
    }

    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfer-notifications", groupId = "notification-svc")
    public void onNotification(TransferNotification event) {
        // Must call back to get the details
        TransferDetails details = restClient.get()
                .uri(event.detailsLink())
                .retrieve()
                .body(TransferDetails.class);

        sendSms(event.accountId(),
                "Transfer of " + details.amount() + " to " + details.beneficiary());
    }

    private void sendSms(String accountId, String message) {
        // Send SMS
    }
}
```

```text
// Notification consumer receives tiny event → calls back for details
// If Transfer Service is down during callback → consumer is stuck
// Runtime coupling: consumer depends on producer being available
```

### What is Event-Carried State Transfer?

Event-Carried State Transfer includes the data consumers need inside the event. The consumer acts without calling back to the producer.

```java
package com.bank.event;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Instant;

// Event-Carried State Transfer: includes the data consumers need
public record TransferCompletedEvent(
        String eventId,
        String transferId,
        String fromAccount,
        String toAccount,
        BigDecimal amount,
        String currency,
        Instant completedAt
) {}

// Example payload:
// {
//   "eventId": "EVT-001",
//   "transferId": "TXN-123",
//   "fromAccount": "SAV2001",
//   "toAccount": "SAV3002",
//   "amount": 50000,
//   "currency": "INR",
//   "completedAt": "2025-01-15T10:30:00Z"
// }
// Payload size: ~250 bytes (larger, but self-contained)
```

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import com.bank.event.TransferCompletedEvent;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class NotificationConsumerAutonomous {

    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfer-events", groupId = "notification-svc")
    public void onTransferCompleted(TransferCompletedEvent event) {
        // No callback needed! All data is in the event.
        sendSms(event.fromAccount(),
                "Transfer of " + event.amount() + " " + event.currency()
                + " to " + event.toAccount() + " completed.");
    }

    private void sendSms(String accountId, String message) {
        // Send SMS to account holder
    }
}
```

```text
// Consumer acts immediately with no callback
// If Transfer Service is down → consumer still works (autonomous)
// Schema coupling: consumer depends on event schema at design time
```

### What is the fat event / god event anti-pattern?

The fat event serializes the entire aggregate and broadcasts it. This leaks internals, bloats the topic, and couples every consumer to the full model.

```java
package com.bank.event;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

// WRONG: fat event / god event
public record TransferGodEvent(
        String eventId,
        String transferId,
        String fromAccount,
        String toAccount,
        BigDecimal amount,
        BigDecimal fee,
        String currency,
        String internalLedgerRef,    // Internal detail!
        String riskBucket,            // Internal detail!
        BigDecimal senderBalance,     // Sensitive!
        BigDecimal receiverBalance,   // Sensitive!
        List<String> auditNotes,      // Internal detail!
        String approvedBy,
        String complianceStatus,
        String fraudScore
) {}
// Payload size: ~800 bytes
// Leaks: internalLedgerRef, riskBucket, balances, audit notes
// Every consumer now coupled to every field
```

```text
// Problems with the god event:
// 1. Leaks internal model: internalLedgerRef, riskBucket
// 2. Bloats topic: 800 bytes vs 250 bytes per event
// 3. Couples consumers: renaming riskBucket breaks everyone
// 4. Obscures contract: nobody knows which fields matter
// 5. Security risk: exposes balances and audit notes
```

### How is Event Notification different from State Transfer? (comparison table)

| Aspect | Event Notification | Event-Carried State Transfer |
|---|---|---|
| Payload | Tiny (ID, type, link) | Medium (curated fields) |
| Consumer action | Calls back for details | Acts immediately |
| Runtime coupling | Consumer depends on producer being up | Consumer is autonomous |
| Design-time coupling | Minimal schema | Consumer depends on event schema |
| Freshness | Always fresh (fetches current state) | Snapshot at emission time |
| Network load | Callback per event | No extra calls |
| Resilience | Fails if producer is down during callback | Works even if producer is down |
| Best for | Large/sensitive data, freshness critical | Autonomy, local read models, fan-out |

### How do you decide between the two styles?

The decision is a trade-off between coupling and autonomy. Favor State Transfer when consumer autonomy matters, when callback load would be heavy, or when consumers maintain local read models.

```java
// Decision framework:

// Use Event-Carried State Transfer when:
// - Many consumers need the same data (fan-out)
// - Consumers maintain local read models (CQRS)
// - Producer availability should not affect consumers
// - Example: transfer event → notification, statement, fraud all need amount

// Use Event Notification when:
// - Data is large (e.g., full PDF statement)
// - Data is sensitive (e.g., full KYC documents)
// - Freshness is critical (e.g., real-time balance check)
// - Few consumers need details (callback cost is low)

// Hybrid: carry common fields + include a link for rare detailed lookups
public record TransferEventHybrid(
        String eventId,
        String transferId,
        String fromAccount,
        String toAccount,
        java.math.BigDecimal amount,    // Commonly needed
        String currency,                 // Commonly needed
        String detailsLink               // For rare full lookups
) {}
```

### What are common mistakes? (wrong vs correct)

The most common mistake is broadcasting the entire aggregate as a god event instead of curating the fields.

```java
// WRONG: god event - dump everything
kafka.send("transfer-events", accountId, godEvent);
// Consumers see: internalLedgerRef, riskBucket, balances
// Any internal rename breaks all consumers

// CORRECT: curated event with only needed fields
TransferCompletedEvent curated = new TransferCompletedEvent(
        eventId, transferId, fromAccount, toAccount, amount, "INR", now);
kafka.send("transfer-events", fromAccount, curated);
// Consumers see: transferId, fromAccount, toAccount, amount, currency
// Internal changes have zero impact on consumers
```

### Do both styles need versioned schemas?

Yes. Both Event Notification and State Transfer create contracts that consumers depend on. Both must use a versioned schema like Avro or Protobuf with a Schema Registry.

```json
// Avro schema for Event-Carried State Transfer
{
  "type": "record",
  "name": "TransferCompleted",
  "namespace": "com.bank.events",
  "fields": [
    {"name": "eventId", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "transferId", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "fromAccount", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "toAccount", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "amount", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "currency", "type": "string", "default": "INR"}
  ]
}
// Adding "beneficiaryName" with a default → backward compatible
// Removing "transferId" → breaking → Schema Registry rejects
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer understands that the choice is about which coupling you prefer. Event Notification couples consumers to the producer at runtime; State Transfer couples them to the producer's schema at design time. You minimize both by keeping events lean, versioned, and focused on the domain fact.

```java
// Senior framing:
// Event Notification: runtime coupling (must call back)
// State Transfer: design-time coupling (must parse schema)
// God event: both couplings at maximum (worst of all worlds)

// The right answer is almost always State Transfer with curated fields
// It gives autonomy, resilience, and works for local read models
// Use Notification only when data is too large or sensitive to carry
```
